n\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/04-experience-contract.md:38:## Story, content, and feedback principles\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/04-experience-contract.md:46:## Layout, visual tokens, and responsive behaviour\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/04-experience-contract.md:68:## Input and interaction adaptation\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/04-experience-contract.md:81:## Accessibility policy\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/04-experience-contract.md:94:## Empty, error, recovery, destructive, and privacy-sensitive states\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/04-experience-contract.md:107:## PWA-specific experience\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/04-experience-contract.md:113:## Content and interaction review checklist\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/04-experience-contract.md:117:## Open decisions retained for approval\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md:9:## Product outcome and personas\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md:24:## Core journeys\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md:36:## Domain vocabulary and state ownership\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md:50:## System relationship map\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md:87:## Accepted domain chapters\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md:89:### 1. Game shell, session, input, and responsive presentation\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md:104:### 2. Exploration, town, dungeon, and campaign content\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md:117:### 3. Narrative, quests, onboarding, and ending\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md:132:### 4. Party combat, encounters, rewards, and balance\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md:146:### 5. Inventory, party progression, and economy\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md:160:### 6. Save, recovery, and offline persistence\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md:175:### 7. Accessibility, localisation, and audiovisual feedback\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md:191:### 8. PWA delivery, release safety, diagnostics, and quality operations\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md:209:## Capability parity and intentional differences\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md:220:## Prioritised feature catalogue and slices\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md:245:## Approval gates and unanswered questions\n---\ntask_id: B0007\nsequence: 10\ntitle: Author campaign domain-state contracts\nbrowser_impact: not_applicable\nplaywright_spec:\n---\n\n## Objective\n\nCreate only `../runbooks/R0013.md` through `../runbooks/R0016.md` from the\nassigned SHARD-02 catalogue contracts. Preserve GATE-0004 as an implementation\ngate; it does not block writing these bounded implementation runbooks.\n\n## Context packet\n\nRead `../PROJECT_BRIEF.md`, the B0007 and assigned R manifest records,\n`../planning/implementation-catalogue-index.json`,\n`../specification/02-feature-model.md#2-exploration-town-dungeon-and-campaign-content`\nthrough `#5-inventory-party-progression-and-economy`, and\n`../specification/03-technical-contract.md#data-contract` plus\n`#commands-validation-idempotency-and-concurrency`. Use `../../../runbooks/TEMPLATE.md`\nand F014 provenance/asset rules.\n\n## Output list\n\n- `../runbooks/R0013.md` \u2014 IMP-0013.\n- `../runbooks/R0014.md` \u2014 IMP-0014.\n- `../runbooks/R0015.md` \u2014 IMP-0015.\n- `../runbooks/R0016.md` \u2014 IMP-0016.\n\n## Validation procedure\n\nBefore writing, confirm each path is absent or is this batch's repair target;\nnever overwrite accepted work. Compare front matter, requirement IDs,\ndependencies, ownership, provenance, factory stages, and asset assessment to\nthe manifest. Include all standard contract sections, `## Verification`, and\n`## Asset assessment`; retain GATE-0004 rather than resolving it.\n\n## Checkpoint and update procedure\n\nAfter each valid R file, record the result in the B0007 checkpoint/state only.\nOn failure retain R0013\u2013R0016 and repair only the failed assigned path, then\nrerun the structural validator through B0007.\n\n## Collision protection\n\nCheck manifest ID/path ownership and the filesystem before every output. Stop\non a duplicate ID, output path, provenance mismatch, or conflicting accepted\ncontract; do not renumber or reallocate.\n\n## Stop condition\n\nStop after these four R outputs. Do not author B0008 outputs, allocate IDs, or\ncreate product assets.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- Exactly R0013 through R0016 are created, each mapping only to its assigned\n  `IMP-*` record.\n- Every generated R file has valid Supervisor metadata, canonical provenance,\n  requirement links, dependency-correct ownership, verification, and an\n  explicit asset/audio assessment.\n- GATE-0004 remains explicit on the relevant R contracts and is never treated\n  as a reason to defer or omit their authoring.\n- The structural validator succeeds through B0007 before this B task is\n  accepted, without overwriting an accepted contract.\n---\ntask_id: C0001\nsequence: 1\ntitle: Validate the bounded foundation catalogue bootstrap\nbrowser_impact: not_applicable\nplaywright_spec:\n---\n\n## Objective\n\nValidate the existing `SHARD-01` implementation catalogue before authoring.\nRead only the canonical specification, `planning/implementation-catalogue-index.json`,\nand `planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.json`. Confirm that SHARD-01 contains\nat most 250 `IMP-*` contracts, that every ready foundation contract is allocated\nonce, and that SHARD-02 through SHARD-05 remain queued or held. Do not expand a\nfuture chapter, create R-series files, or change accepted IDs.\n\n## Validation procedure\n\nCheck unique `IMP-*`, B, and R IDs; contract-to-manifest coverage; dependency\nclosure; the 250-contract bound; and that B0001 through B0006 each contain no\nmore than seven R IDs. Record only factual repairs to the manifest/ledger when\nthe sources agree. Stop and report any missing canonical source, conflicting\nownership, cyclic dependency, or attempt to allocate a held connected-scope\nchapter.\n\n## Checkpoint and update procedure\n\nSet the manifest wave checkpoint only after the structural checks pass. Preserve\nall counters and existing allocations; append a short checkpoint note to the\nledger if it does not already describe the result. Leave future allocation to\nD0001.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- SHARD-01 is verified as a bounded catalogue of no more than 250 contracts.\n- The twelve foundation `IMP-*` records are each allocated exactly once to B0001 through B0006, with no batch over seven R contracts.\n- Queued and held chapters remain unexpanded and no R-series implementation runbook is created.\n- Conflicts, missing sources, cycles, or ID collisions stop the task without renumbering or overwriting work.\n# Runbook-authoring allocation ledger\n\nThis readable companion to [`runbook-authoring-manifest.json`](runbook-authoring-manifest.json) is the persistent allocation ledger for the mock-fantasy-quest-4 authoring system. The JSON is authoritative for IDs, provenance, dependencies, output paths, counters, and recovery state. Accepted IDs are immutable: resume from the first non-accepted batch and append only at `B0007` and `R0013`.\n\n## Dispatcher checkpoint \u2014 D0002\n\nD0002 expanded the now-authorable `SHARD-02` exactly once into eight contracts\n(`IMP-0013`\u2013`IMP-0020`), below its 250-record limit. `GATE-0004` remains on\neach affected contract as an implementation gate and was not treated as an\nauthoring blocker. The eight contracts are allocated once between B0007 and\nB0008 (four outputs each), with asset reservation only in R0018. SHARD-03 and\nSHARD-04 remain queued, so [`D0003`](../authoring-runbooks/D0003.md) is the\nsole successor. Counters now append at B0009/R0021; accepted work is unchanged.\n\n## Dispatcher checkpoint \u2014 D0001\n\nD0001 expanded no chapter and allocated no B/R contracts. `SHARD-02` is the\nfirst queued chapter, but `DEC-0004`/`GATE-0004` remains unresolved; `SHARD-03`\nand `SHARD-04` retain their predecessor and gate blocks, while `SHARD-05` stays\nheld pending explicit authorisation. Every current catalogue contract\n(`IMP-0001` through `IMP-0012`) is allocated exactly once. The dispatcher\ntherefore created [`D0002`](../authoring-runbooks/D0002.md) as the sole\nsuccessor, without advancing B/R counters or modifying accepted work.\n\n## Wave 1 \u2014 foundation contracts\n\n| Order | Batch | R contracts (maximum 7) | Dependency / parallel disposition | Checkpoint |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | B0001 | R0001 | GATE-0001; no parallel work | Record gate and R0001 ownership |\n| 2 | B0002 | R0002, R0003, R0010 | After B0001 | Validate seams, fixtures, tokens and retained gates |\n| 3 | B0003 | R0004, R0008 | After B0002 | Validate state slice and P0 service exclusion |\n| 4 | B0004 | R0005, R0006 | After B0003; R0006 waits for GATE-0003 | Validate content/save separation |\n| 5 | B0005 | R0007, R0009, R0011 | After B0004; R0007/GATE-0003 and R0009/GATE-0005 remain blocked | Validate migration, diagnostics, provenance |\n| 6 | B0006 | R0012 | After B0003 and B0004; safe in parallel with B0005 after B0004 | Cross-check foundation documentation and gates |\n| 7 | B0007 | R0013, R0014, R0015, R0016 | After B0003; GATE-0004 retained | Validate domain-state seams and non-asset boundaries |\n| 8 | B0008 | R0017, R0018, R0019, R0020 | After B0007; GATE-0004/provenance retained | Validate campaign integration, asset reservation, and SHARD-03 handoff |\n\nThe batches are deliberately smaller than seven where a contract is context-heavy (state, persistence, content) or tightly coupled to its predecessor. Together they form an incremental foundation vertical slice: reproducible baseline, shared seams/test harness, deterministic game state and P0 exclusion, content and recovery boundaries, then provenance/diagnostic controls and documented closure. They do not imply that the game is implemented.\n\n## Contract allocation and asset assessment\n\n| R ID | IMP source | Writer | Output | Asset assessment |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| R0001 | IMP-0001 | B0001 | `runbooks/R0001.md` | not applicable \u2014 tooling boundary only |\n| R0002 | IMP-0002 | B0002 | `runbooks/R0002.md` | not applicable \u2014 interfaces only |\n| R0003 | IMP-0003 | B0002 | `runbooks/R0003.md` | not applicable \u2014 deterministic fixtures only |\n| R0004 | IMP-0004 | B0003 | `runbooks/R0004.md` | not applicable \u2014 state envelopes only |\n| R0005 | IMP-0005 | B0004 | `runbooks/R0005.md` | not applicable \u2014 schema reserves later asset fields |\n| R0006 | IMP-0006 | B0004 | `runbooks/R0006.md` | not applicable \u2014 save/recovery protocol |\n| R0007 | IMP-0007 | B0005 | `runbooks/R0007.md` | not applicable \u2014 migrations only |\n| R0008 | IMP-0008 | B0003 | `runbooks/R0008.md` | not applicable \u2014 negative service scope |\n| R0009 | IMP-0009 | B0005 | `runbooks/R0009.md` | not applicable \u2014 local diagnostics only |\n| R0010 | IMP-0010 | B0002 | `runbooks/R0010.md` | not applicable \u2014 no final art/fonts/icons |\n| R0011 | IMP-0011 | B0005 | `runbooks/R0011.md` | not applicable \u2014 provenance control only |\n| R0012 | IMP-0012 | B0006 | `runbooks/R0012.md` | not applicable \u2014 documentation only |\n| R0013\u2013R0017, R0019\u2013R0020 | IMP-0013\u2013IMP-0017, IMP-0019\u2013IMP-0020 | B0007/B0008 | `runbooks/R0013.md`\u2013`R0020.md` | not applicable \u2014 contract, fixture, integration, and documentation work only |\n| R0018 | IMP-0018 | B0008 | `runbooks/R0018.md` | required \u2014 reserves original campaign asset groups; authoring must not create them |\n\nNo Wave 1 contract creates a product asset. Each contract still carries an explicit `asset_impact`, empty `asset_ids`, and an asset brief in the JSON; its author must prohibit incidental asset creation. A later asset-required contract must reserve stable product-specific IDs and a brief before assignment.\n\n## Author packet and resume rules\n\nEach B writer receives only the baseline terminology, target constraints, templates, authoring rules, and the named specification/catalogue records in its JSON `context_packet`. Every R record has canonical `source_specifications`, `source_catalogue_ids`, `authoring_batch`, and `factory_stages` provenance. Use [`runbooks/TEMPLATE.md`](../../../runbooks/TEMPLATE.md) plus the F014 metadata and asset requirements.\n\nAt every checkpoint, validate unique B/R IDs, output-path ownership, dependency closure, provenance, the batch maximum, asset assessment, and retained human gates. On failure, retain all assigned IDs, repair the bounded failed output, and resume that same batch. Never regenerate accepted contracts or renumber the ledger.\n\n## Queued expansion chapters\n\n| Chapter | Future allocation point | State |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| SHARD-02 \u2014 Campaign content and domain logic | Wave 2 authoring allocation; GATE-0004 retained for implementation | expanded |\n| SHARD-03 \u2014 Session, UI, input, and accessibility | Wave 3 after SHARD-02 and GATE-0002/0003/0004 | queued |\n| SHARD-04 \u2014 PWA delivery, performance, operations, release evidence | Wave 4 after SHARD-02/03 and GATE-0002/0005/0007 | queued |\n| SHARD-05 \u2014 Connected-scope decision package | Only explicit authorisation after GATE-0006; no P0 R allocation | hold |\n\nExpand one queued chapter into catalogue records before allocating its R IDs. This preserves the catalogue\u2019s dependency graph and makes a future thousand-runbook programme resumable without changing accepted work.\n\n## Structural check\n\n```sh\nnode -e 'const m=require(\"./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.json\"); const r=m.r_contracts, b=m.batches, ids=a=>new Set(a).size===a.length; if (!ids(r.map(x=>x.id)) || !ids(b.map(x=>x.id)) || r.length!==m.expected_runbook_count || b.some(x=>x.r_contract_ids.length>7) || r.some(x=>!x.source_specifications.length || !x.source_catalogue_ids.length || !x.authoring_batch || !x.factory_stages.length || ![\"required\",\"not_applicable\"].includes(x.asset_impact) || !x.output_path || (x.asset_impact===\"required\" && (!x.asset_ids.length || !x.asset_brief))) ) process.exit(1); console.log(\"runbook authoring ledger structural check passed\")'\n```\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,100p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/implementation-catalogue-index.json; tail -100 projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/implementation-catalogue-index.json; sed -n '84,130p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md; sed -n '46,120p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/04-experience-contract.md\" in /Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator\n succeeded in 0ms:\n{\n  \"catalogue_id\": \"mock-fantasy-quest-4-implementation-catalogue\",\n  \"version\": 1,\n  \"source\": [\"../specification/README.md\", \"../specification/requirements.json\", \"05-delivery-map.md\", \"06-foundation-domain-contracts.md\", \"07-feature-experience-contracts.md\", \"08-quality-operations-contracts.md\"],\n  \"scope\": \"P0 is an original, offline-capable 2D single-player fantasy RPG delivered through a shared web core and a PWA delivery adaptation. Connected services are not P0 implementation scope.\",\n  \"first_bounded_domain\": {\"id\": \"SHARD-01\", \"title\": \"Foundation and core contracts\", \"contract_limit\": 250, \"contract_count\": 12, \"state\": \"ready_for_authoring\", \"boundary\": \"Framework-neutral seams, deterministic state/data contracts, local persistence protocol, qua